traduku (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Windows 10/11 is a platform this app ships for. traduku.exe is built GUI
    subsystem (no console window behind a tray app), the gesture is a
    WH_KEYBOARD_LL passive hook (RegisterHotKey was rejected: it intercepts the
    copy the user is making), the clipboard is Qt's win32 one, notifications are
    Shell_NotifyIcon balloons, the tray is QSystemTrayIcon, autostart is the
    HKCU Run value verified by an independent reader, and the artifact is a
    portable zip that runs with no Qt and no compiler installed.
  * The Windows cues are audible. A Qt 6 QMediaPlayer without a QAudioOutput
    plays to nobody, and the one sound people heard was Windows' own balloon
    chime. The player is wired to a speaker, the audio device is opened before
    the first gesture, and --backend-info prints the device and the file behind
    every cue - so "no sound" and "there is no sound device" are two different
    answers.
  * The start cue is a sound a person chose by ear, selected from the Windows
    media files by measurement (audible span, loudness of the loudest 100 ms),
    not by filename.
  * Sound has two switches: one for the beginning of a translation, one for the
    end. One box labelled "starts / finishes" carried one bit, so muting the
    start cue - the request the desktop walk produced - had no control at all.
  * Choosing a destination language now translates. The window's list and the
    tray's "Translate To" save the language and translate the text that is on
    screen, the way the Ctrl+C twice gesture does: a language picked over a pane
    full of text is a question about that text. The list in the Settings dialog
    stays a form field - it applies on OK, and Cancel cancels.
  * The tray menu is a view, not a second writer of the settings file, and the
    tick on the language row follows the language the app has rather than the
    last row clicked.

 -- Traduku contributors <dev@localhost>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:15:00 +0000

traduku (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * GNOME shell: the app now runs on GNOME/Wayland without any KF6 - the
    gesture reads /dev/input (no keyboard grab, works under Wayland), the
    clipboard is wl-clipboard, notifications go over the freedesktop D-Bus
    service, the tray is Qt's own StatusNotifierItem client.
  * One tray for both desktops. KdeTray (KF6 KStatusNotifierItem) is removed:
    the Qt tray reproduces its menu entry for entry in Plasma, and KF6's item
    segfaulted when destroyed with a live session bus - which is what tray ->
    Quit did in 0.2 (the KDE application crashed on exit).
  * Packaging is now two flavours from one source: traduku (Plasma, KF6) and
    traduku-gnome (GNOME, no KF6, wl-clipboard as a hard dependency, and the
    udev uaccess rule the /dev/input gesture needs to get an ACL - no user is
    added to the input group). The previous .deb built the Plasma shell on
    every distribution, so the GNOME shell could not be installed at all.
  * API failures are reported in words: a server that is not running, a wrong
    key (401/403), a wrong URL or model (404), a dead gateway (5xx), an HTML
    page answering where JSON was expected, and a 200 that carried no text
    each say what to check and name the endpoint. They used to come out as
    "HTTP status 0" or "the model returned an empty translation".
  * Reasoning models no longer stall the translation: known "think less"
    request options are sent by default (DisableReasoning, ReasoningOffOptions),
    ExtraOptions merges arbitrary server-specific fields, and a gateway that
    rejects unknown fields costs a retry, not the translation.
  * Configuration can be pointed at another endpoint for one run: --config
    <path> and $TRADUKU_CONFIG, without moving the file out of
    ~/.config/traduku/config.ini.
  * Clipboard failure is actionable: on Wayland without wl-clipboard the app
    names the missing package instead of reporting an empty clipboard.
  * Clipboard translation works from a background process on Wayland
    (wl-paste), which Qt's QClipboard cannot do.

 -- Traduku contributors <dev@localhost>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0100

traduku (0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Renamed project: llm-translate -> Traduku (binary `traduku`, config
    ~/.config/traduku/config.ini, autostart entry, D-Bus name
    dev.traduku.Traduku). No migration code: pre-release, no users to move.
  * Version kept in one place: CMakeLists VERSION is the source of truth and
    the changelog follows it.
  * Build-Depends: accept Ubuntu's Qt6 Core5Compat package name
    (qt6-5compat-dev) as an alternative to Debian's (libqt6core5compat6-dev).

 -- Traduku contributors <dev@localhost>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

traduku (0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release: double Ctrl+C clipboard translation with streaming,
    interactive window, incremental retranslation, sounds, tray,
    first-run guided setup (autostart question + settings dialog).

